>> title: Palak Paneer
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: paneer, spinach, indian, vegetarian, curry
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: palak panir, saag paneer, spinach paneer, palak-paneer, saag panir
>> pairs-with: paneer, naan, pilau-rice
>> servings: 4
>> time: 50 min
>> step.1: steam 2 min, then run under cold water
>> step.2: blend to a coarse purée
>> step.3: sweat 8 min
>> step.4: fry in 2 min
>> step.5: simmer 6 min, until the tomato goes to pulp
>> step.6: fold in, simmer 5 min

Steam @spinach{1%lb}(450 g; stems and all, washed) with @green chiles{2}(slit) and @garlic{2%cloves} in a covered #pot{} ~steam{2%min}, then tip it into a colander and run it under cold water until it is cold to the hand.

Blend @&(~1)wilted spinach{} in a #blender{} to a coarse purée, stopping while there is still texture in it — palak is not a soup and should not be smooth.

Sweat @yellow onion{1%medium}(finely chopped) in @ghee{3%Tbs}(42 g) in a #heavy pan{} ~fry{8%min}.

Fry @ginger-garlic paste{1%Tbs}(17 g), @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @ground coriander{1%tsp} and @garam masala{1/2%tsp} into @&(~1)softened onion{} ~fry{2%min}.

Simmer @tomato{1%large}(chopped) and @kosher salt{1 1/4%tsp} with @&(~1)masala{} ~simmer{6%min}, until the tomato has gone to pulp.

Fold @&(~4)spinach purée{} into @&(~1)masala{} with @paneer{12%oz}(340 g; cut in 3/4-in cubes), @heavy cream{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @kasuri methi{1%tsp}(crumbled), and ~simmer{5%min} on low.

The cheese goes in raw here and stays soft. Fried gold in ghee then soaked in hot water is the other way.
